    id: "d102dd63a2f33b01e8b7",
    cat: "conflitti",
    title: "Major Russian grain export terminals hit in Ukraine Black Sea port attack",
    summary: "Grain exports from both Russia and Ukraine are falling as both countries attack each other's shipping facilities and ports.",
    preview: "A \"massive\" Ukrainian drone and missile strike on Russia's port of Novorossiysk has damaged two major grain export terminals and hit the last big Russian naval base on the Black Sea, reports say. Three people, including an eight-year-old child, were killed and another 24 injured in the overnight attack, local officials said. Ukraine's President Volodymyr Zelensky said Kyiv had launched rockets, jet and naval drones in a \"unique operation\" to strike the Russian base, although he did not mention grain terminals being hit.",
    body: "A \"massive\" Ukrainian drone and missile strike on Russia's port of Novorossiysk has damaged two major grain export terminals and hit the last big Russian naval base on the Black Sea, reports say.\n\nThree people, including an eight-year-old child, were killed and another 24 injured in the overnight attack, local officials said.\n\nUkraine's President Volodymyr Zelensky said Kyiv had launched rockets, jet and naval drones in a \"unique operation\" to strike the Russian base, although he did not mention grain terminals being hit.\n\nUkraine's military later said four Russian warships, including two frigates, were hit. Russia has not commented on the issue.\n\n\"The occupying fleet and all the infrastructure that supports it will not be safe as long as Russian aggression continues,\" Zelensky said in a post on social media.\n\nMost of Russia's Black Sea fleet is docked in Novorossiysk - one of the largest ports in Russia - after it was forced to vacate its former home base of Sevastopol, in the Russia-occupied Crimean peninsula, which was too exposed to Ukrainian attacks.\n\nThe attack follows an uptick in the exchange of strikes in the Black Sea this summer, after a period of relative calm.\n\nA major grain trading company confirmed to Russia's TASS news agency that its terminal had been hit in the attack on Wednesday, while another told Reuters and Russian outlet RBK the same.\n\nThe Russian agriculture ministry announced on Wednesday that it was working to redirect cargo flows to ports in the Baltic and Caspian Seas as well as land routes. It did not make any reference to Ukraine's attacks but said it was \"taking into account current logistical constraints\".\n\nRepeated explosions reportedly rang out over Novorossiysk throughout the night as air defences shot down aerial objects.\n\nAn emergency situation was declared in the city on Wednesday, after the barrage of drones struck areas around the port and damaged pipelines, suspending water supply for the city.\n\nElsewhere in the region a man was killed by drone debris, regional Governor Veniamin Kondratyev said. Several locations in the surrounding Krasnodar region were also struck by \"several hundred drones\" overnight, Kondratyev added, with dozens of buildings damaged, including homes.\n\nFor its part, Moscow launched aerial overnight strikes which killed at least 11 civilians in Ukraine between Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nThe majority were killed when Russia hit the southern city of Zaporizhzhia with ballistic missiles and glide bombs.\n\nIn an interview with CNN, Zelensky said that Ukraine would need a tenth of the current US stockpile of interceptor missiles to stop all Russian ballistic missile attacks. Five percent of the stockpile would get Ukraine through winter, he added.\n\nHe said this year Ukraine has two and a half times fewer interceptors than it did in 2025, while \"Russia has two times more ballistic missiles per month than they had before.\"\n\nRussian President Vladimir Putin launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022.\n\nThe last remaining sea route out of Ukraine has been badly affected by several deadly Russian strikes on cargo ships and the port of Odesa, through which Moscow seeks to put off foreign companies from trading with Ukraine.\n\nIn July, the Ukrainian Agribusiness Club (UCAB) reported a 23% decrease in exports.\n\nMeanwhile, Ukraine has grown increasingly adept at carrying out long-range strikes on Russian territory. Its recent attacks on vessels in the eastern Black Sea have disrupted Russia's grain shipments, too.\n\nRussia is the world's largest wheat exporter - but according to a Moscow-based analytics company, exports for August could fall by more than half compared to the same month over the last five years due to the disruption in the Black and Azov seas.\n\nOn Wednesday, Russian foreign ministry spokeswoman Maria Zakharova accused Ukraine of carrying out strikes on Russian grain and agricultural infrastructure, resulting in a hike in global food prices and growing grain shortages.\n\nBut Zakharova also said that Russia would continue striking \"facilities that [Ukraine's armed forces] use on the Black Sea to destabilise the navigation situation\" until \"the full elimination of threats to security projected to the Sea of Azov and the Black Sea\".\n\nConcerns over how the war in Ukraine would affect global food supplies have been persistent since the start of Russia's invasion in 2022.\n\nTogether, Russia and Ukraine account for a significant share of global grain trade, particularly for buyers in North Africa, the Middle East and parts of Asia.\n\nThe 2022 Black Sea Grain Initiative, under which Russia agreed to lift its blockade on Ukraine's agricultural exports, did not last after Moscow resumed attacks on vessels using the maritime corridors to Odesa the following year.\n\nRelated topics\n\n- Published6 August\n\n- Published3 days ago",
